\begin{verbatim}
N=Q1||Q2;
expdim=#Md - (rank Q1) - (rank Q2);
actdim=#Md-(rank N);
if actdim > expdim and actdim > 0 then 
     print {"n=",n,"d=",d,"m=",m,"edim=",expdim,"adim=",actdim}}}}
\end{verbatim}
It's possible the script would run faster by evaluating the matrix $Q_2$ of partials at a random point
(with coordinates in the rationals or even in the integers)
rather than at a generic point. To do so, replace the line\\
\verb!apply(Md,i->(N={};for s from 0 to #Mm-1 do N=N|{diff(Mm_s,i)};D=D|{N}));!\\
with
\begin{verbatim}
G={};
v={};
F=random(1,R);
for i from 0 to n do (v=v|{diff(x_i,F)});
G=G|{v};
apply(Md,i->(N={};for s from 0 to #Mm-1 do 
     N=N|{sub(diff(Mm_s,i),matrix G)};D=D|{N}));
\end{verbatim}
By semicontinuity, if the script indicates that there is no
unexpected hypersurface for a given $Z$, $n$, $d$ and $m$ (by not outputting anything),
then there is indeed no such unexpected hypersurface. But output claiming
an unexpected hypersurface can't be relied on since the random point might
have been unlucky.

